<span class="eyebrow">FAQ</span>
<h2>What buyers still want to know</h2>
</div>
<div>
<div class="faq-list"><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>How do I avoid buying the wrong beard trimmer length system?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Start with the beard length you actually keep most often. If you mostly trim short stubble, fine dial control matters. If you maintain a normal short-to-medium beard, stable guards and repeatable length settings matter more than tiny increments.</p></details><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>Can a beard trimmer replace an electric shaver?</summary><p class="faq-answer">No. A beard trimmer is for controlling hair length, shaping edges, and maintaining facial hair. An electric shaver is for removing hair close to the skin. A trimmer can tidy a beard, but it will not give the same close shave.</p></details><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>What matters more: motor power or blade quality?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Both matter, but blade quality and guard stability usually decide the daily result. A strong motor still gives uneven trims if the guards flex, the blades dull quickly, or the cutting head pulls coarse hair.</p></details><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>Is waterproofing important on a beard trimmer?</summary><p class="faq-answer">It helps, but it is not the whole decision. Waterproof or washable designs make cleanup easier and reduce maintenance friction, but they do not automatically mean better cutting or longer life.</p></details><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>How often do beard trimmer blades need maintenance?</summary><p class="faq-answer">It depends on the design. Some Philips-style self-sharpening systems are relatively low-maintenance, while other trimmers may need regular oiling. The important thing is not to ignore hair buildup, because it makes even good blades feel weaker over time.</p></details><details class="faq-item"><summary>Why do cheap beard trimmers start pulling hair?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Usually because of dull blades, weak motors, poor guard fit, or battery fade. Pulling is one of the clearest signs that the trimmer is no longer cutting cleanly under real use.</p></details><details class="faq-item"><summary>Should I buy a separate detail trimmer?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Only if you care about sharp lineups or very precise edging. Most people can handle normal neckline, sideburn, and beard cleanup with a good main trimmer, but a dedicated detailer can help if clean edges are the priority.</p></details><details class="faq-item"><summary>What is the safest way to think about attachments?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Treat attachments as useful only after the core trimmer is proven. A smaller set of stable guards is better than a large kit full of pieces that feel loose, duplicate each other, or never get used.</p></details>
</div>
